CertHub.register({"id":"sc-200","vendor":"Microsoft","name":"Microsoft Certified: Security Operations Analyst Associate","short":"SC-200","exam":"SC-200","blurb":"Security operations with Microsoft Defender XDR, Microsoft Defender for Cloud and Microsoft Sentinel: configuring detections, responding to incidents and hunting with KQL, for SOC analysts.","status":"check","statusNote":"Could not open learn.microsoft.com directly on Sept 24, 2026 (blocked from this build environment), so the weights come from search results that quote the official SC-200 study guide. Those results say the skills measured were restructured on April 16, 2026 and refreshed on July 28, 2026 into three groups: Manage a security operations environment (40–45%), Respond to security incidents (35–40%) and Perform threat hunting (20–25%). The midpoints (42.5, 37.5, 22.5) were scaled to sum to 100 and rounded to 41/37/22. Microsoft's pages also list an update to the English exam on October 21, 2026. No retirement or replacement announcement was found. Before you book, check the study guide for the current percentages and change log.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[{"from":"2026-09-24","until":"2026-10-21","text":"Microsoft lists an update to the English SC-200 exam on October 21, 2026. Compare the new 'skills measured' list in the official study guide with this plan before your exam date. The exam is still available, and no retirement has been announced."}],"examInfo":{"questions":"Not published (Microsoft associate exams usually have about 40–60 questions, sometimes with case studies)","minutes":100,"pass":"700 on a 1–1000 scale"},"examSim":{"questions":50,"minutes":100},"sources":[{"label":"Microsoft Learn: SC-200 study guide (skills measured)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/sc-200"},{"label":"Microsoft Learn: Security Operations Analyst Associate certification page","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/security-operations-analyst/"},{"label":"Microsoft Learn: SC-200T00 course","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/courses/sc-200t00"}],"planWeeks":10,"hoursPerWeek":"6–8","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Manage a security operations environment","w":41,"topics":["Defender XDR settings: incident and alert email notifications, alert tuning (suppression) rules, portal RBAC and device groups","Defender for Endpoint configuration: onboarding, device groups, tamper protection, attack surface reduction rules (audit, warn, block), indicators and network protection, device discovery","Defender for Cloud: foundational CSPM vs paid workload protection plans (Servers, Storage, Databases), connecting AWS and GCP accounts, Defender for Endpoint integration for servers","Defender for Identity sensors on domain controllers and AD FS/AD CS servers; Defender for Office 365 Safe Links and Safe Attachments","Sentinel workspace design: Log Analytics workspace, Sentinel roles (Reader, Responder, Contributor, Automation Contributor), onboarding to the Defender portal","Data retention and cost: analytics tier vs Sentinel data lake tier, table plans, summary rules, SOC optimization recommendations","Data connectors and Content hub solutions; Windows Security Events and CEF/Syslog through the Azure Monitor Agent and data collection rules (DCRs); the Logs Ingestion API for custom sources","Analytics rules: scheduled, near-real-time (NRT), Microsoft incident creation, anomaly and Fusion; entity mapping, alert grouping, custom details","Custom detection rules in Defender XDR advanced hunting; MITRE ATT&CK coverage of your rules","Automation: automation rules vs Logic Apps playbooks, triggers, incident tasks; watchlists, workbooks, UEBA and threat intelligence connectors"],"notes":["Skills measured group 1 (40–45%): configure Defender XDR settings, manage assets and environments, design and configure a Sentinel workspace, ingest data, configure detections and automation"],"labs":["Create an Azure free account, add a Log Analytics workspace and turn on Microsoft Sentinel (check the current free-trial terms for new workspaces). Install the Windows Security Events solution from Content hub and connect one small test VM through the Azure Monitor Agent, using a DCR that collects only event IDs 4624, 4625 and 4688. Cost warning: the VM and any ingestion beyond the trial allowance are billed. Set a budget alert and delete the resource group when you finish.","In that workspace, write a scheduled analytics rule that fires when one account has 5 or more failed logons (4625) within 10 minutes. Map the Account and Host entities, turn on alert grouping, then trigger the rule by entering wrong passwords on your own test VM.","Create an automation rule that assigns your rule's incidents to you, raises their severity and adds three incident tasks. Optionally, add a Consumption Logic App playbook that posts the incident title to a Teams chat. Logic Apps bill per action, so keep it small and delete it afterwards."]},{"id":2,"name":"Respond to security incidents","w":37,"topics":["Defender portal incident queue: triage, assignment, attack story, alert correlation, linking alerts and merging incidents, classification and determination","Defender for Endpoint response: isolate device, restrict app execution, run antivirus scan, collect investigation package, live response, stop and quarantine file, file indicators, device timeline","Action center: pending and completed remediation actions; automatic attack disruption of compromised users and devices","Defender for Office 365: Threat Explorer, removing delivered phishing, user-reported messages and Submissions","Defender for Identity alerts: DCSync, Golden Ticket, pass-the-hash; lateral movement paths; KRBTGT reset","Microsoft Entra ID Protection: risky users and sign-ins, confirm user compromised, revoking sessions; MFA fatigue response","Defender for Cloud Apps: impossible travel and other anomaly alerts, OAuth app risk and revoking app consent","Microsoft Purview: DLP and insider risk alerts in the Defender portal; Purview Audit (unified audit log) searches","Defender for Cloud security alerts: alert details, the Take action tab, triggering automation","Sentinel incidents: investigation graph, entity pages and UEBA insights, running playbooks on demand, incident tasks, closing with the right classification","Microsoft Security Copilot embedded in the Defender portal: incident summaries, guided response, script analysis"],"notes":["Skills measured group 2 (35–40%): respond to alerts and incidents in Defender XDR, Defender for Endpoint, Defender for Cloud, Defender for Cloud Apps, Defender for Identity, Entra ID, Purview and Sentinel, including investigating incidents with agentic AI such as embedded Security Copilot"],"labs":["Start a Microsoft 365 E5 or Defender for Endpoint trial in a separate test tenant (trials last about 30 days and may ask for a payment method; cancel before they convert). Onboard one test VM, run the benign detection test command shown on the onboarding page, then work through the incident: attack story, device timeline, isolate the device and release it.","In the same trial, use Attack simulation training to send a simulated phishing email to a test user. Find the message in Threat Explorer and practice the soft-delete remediation, then check the result in the Action center.","Take one incident from your Sentinel or Defender lab and write a one-page report covering the timeline, affected entities, containment steps, classification and lessons learned, as you would hand it to a Tier-2 analyst."]},{"id":3,"name":"Perform threat hunting","w":22,"topics":["KQL basics: where, project, extend, summarize, count, bin, ago(), order by, take, render","KQL for hunting: has vs contains, in and has_any, let statements and dynamic lists, join kinds, union, parse_json and mv-expand, make-series with anomaly functions","Advanced hunting schema: DeviceProcessEvents, DeviceNetworkEvents, DeviceLogonEvents, EmailEvents, EmailUrlInfo, IdentityLogonEvents, CloudAppEvents, AlertInfo and AlertEvidence","Turning a hunting query into a custom detection rule; Security Copilot help with writing KQL","Sentinel hunting: hunting queries, hunts, bookmarks, livestream, notebooks with MSTICPy","Long-term data: search jobs, restore, Sentinel data lake KQL jobs","Normalized hunting with ASIM parsers across vendors","Threat intelligence: TI indicators, TAXII feeds, threat analytics reports in Defender XDR","Graph-based hunting: Sentinel graph and hunting graphs with blast radius"],"notes":["Skills measured group 3 (20–25%): hunt with KQL in Defender XDR and Sentinel, analyze attack vectors and threat intelligence, use Sentinel graph, data lake KQL jobs and notebooks"],"labs":["Open the free Log Analytics demo workspace (aka.ms/LADemo, no subscription needed) and write queries on SecurityEvent and SigninLogs that use where, summarize with bin(TimeGenerated, 1h), join and render timechart.","Work through the first cases of the free Kusto Detective Agency (detective.kusto.io) or the free 'help' cluster in Azure Data Explorer to practice has vs contains, let, parse and make-series.","In your trial tenant's advanced hunting, write a query for encoded PowerShell command lines in DeviceProcessEvents, save it, then turn it into a custom detection rule. Cost warning: Sentinel search jobs and data lake queries are billed by the data they scan, so try them only on small time ranges."]}],"study":{"1":[["When would you use an automation rule instead of a playbook in Sentinel?","Use automation rules for simple changes built into Sentinel: assigning an owner, changing status or severity, adding tags or tasks, and suppressing noise. Use a playbook (Logic App) when you need to call outside services, such as enrichment APIs, Teams or ticketing systems, or run multi-step logic. An automation rule is often what triggers the playbook."],["Why does entity mapping matter in an analytics rule?","Mapped entities (account, host, IP, URL, file hash) turn raw results into structured evidence. They feed the investigation graph, entity pages and UEBA, allow alert grouping by entity, and let incidents be correlated. Without them the analyst gets text but nothing to pivot on."],["Compare the analytics tier and the Sentinel data lake tier for log storage.","The analytics tier costs more but supports fast interactive queries, analytics rules and workbooks. The data lake tier is cheap long-term storage for high-volume or rarely queried logs. You query it with KQL jobs or notebooks and can promote results back to the analytics tier."],["How do you keep a noisy Windows Security Events connector affordable?","Use a data collection rule with a smaller event set or a custom XPath filter, collect only from servers that matter, move verbose tables to cheaper tiers, and check SOC optimization and usage workbooks for data that no rule uses."],["What should you do before switching an attack surface reduction rule to block mode?","Deploy it in audit mode first, review the audit events in the ASR report or advanced hunting to find legitimate apps it would block, add narrow exclusions if needed, then roll out block mode in stages."]],"2":[["Walk through the first 15 minutes of a Defender XDR incident involving a phished user and a compromised laptop.","Read the incident summary and attack story, confirm the scope (users, devices, mailboxes), isolate the device, confirm the user as compromised or reset the password and revoke sessions, remove the phishing message from other mailboxes, look for inbox rules and new OAuth consents, then document and assign follow-up."],["What's the difference between isolating a device and restricting app execution?","Isolation cuts the device off from the network except for its connection to the Defender service, which stops lateral movement and command-and-control. Restrict app execution keeps the network connection but allows only Microsoft-signed code to run. Use it when the user must keep working and the threat is an unsigned tool."],["Why isn't a password reset enough after a session token has been stolen?","Existing refresh tokens and session cookies can stay valid after a password change. You must also revoke the user's sessions (refresh tokens) so the attacker has to sign in again, and require MFA or a compliant device where possible."],["How do you respond to a suspected Golden Ticket alert from Defender for Identity?","Treat the domain as compromised: find how the KRBTGT hash was obtained, reset the KRBTGT password twice with a replication interval between resets, investigate the privileged accounts involved, and hunt for persistence."],["How should an alert from an authorized penetration test be classified?","As a true alert for expected activity: in Defender XDR, informational/expected activity with a security testing determination; in Sentinel, benign positive. This keeps metrics honest, and the rule itself is not wrong."]],"3":[["Explain the difference between has and contains in KQL.","has matches whole terms using the term index, so it is fast. contains matches any substring and has to scan text, so it is slower. Use has for words such as 'mimikatz' or '-enc', and contains only when you need a partial match inside a term."],["Write the idea of a query that counts failed sign-ins per user per hour and shows the top 10.","SigninLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(1d) and ResultType != \"0\" | summarize Failures = count() by UserPrincipalName, bin(TimeGenerated, 1h) | top 10 by Failures."],["When would you use a search job, and when a data lake KQL job?","A search job scans a table's long-term data for matching records and saves them into a new results table you can query. A data lake KQL job runs a KQL query over data lake data, often on a schedule, and writes the results to an analytics-tier table for detections and investigation."],["What do Sentinel bookmarks do during a hunt?","They save interesting query results with notes, tags and mapped entities. You can then add them to an existing incident or create a new incident from them, so the evidence is kept after the hunt ends."],["Why use ASIM parsers for hunting?","ASIM normalizes data from different vendors into common schemas (such as network session, DNS and authentication), so one query like _Im_NetworkSession works across many firewalls without rewriting it for each product."]]},"qCount":92,"hasLessons":true,"hasLessonsEs":true,"hasPbqs":true,"hasHandson":true});
